{# Row actions collected behind ONE control, that open with JavaScript off.

   The app's own row-action dropdown (`.row-action-menu` / `.row-action-toggle` /
   `.row-action-dropdown`, app.css:6409-6462) is driven entirely by app.js:4220: the panel is
   `display: none` until a delegated click handler copies its HTML into a body-level clone and
   shows that. With scripting off it never opens, so every action behind it is unreachable — which
   is why a screen whose actions today are plain visible buttons cannot simply adopt it. The app
   works without JavaScript (`public/assets/js/app.js`, opening comment); a menu that does not is
   not a menu.

   So the disclosure is `<details>`/`<summary>`, which the browser opens on its own, and the panel
   keeps `.row-action-dropdown` so it inherits every bit of the core styling. The summary is
   deliberately NOT given `.row-action-toggle`: that class is what app.js binds to, and a summary
   carrying it would be opened twice — once natively and once as a body-level clone.

   The panel opens IN FLOW rather than absolutely positioned. Core's version has to reparent itself
   to `<body>` precisely because `.table-scroll-region` has `overflow: auto` and would clip an
   absolutely-positioned panel on the last row; opening in flow makes the row taller instead, which
   nothing can clip and which needs no script to place.

   `display: contents` on a nested `<form>` lets a POST action (a delete) sit in the panel as a real
   submit button and still be laid out as one of the menu's items.

   In-flow is right where something COULD clip the panel — every direct caller of this partial
   today (unit_of_measure, custom_field, tracking_policy) is a row inside one of those scrolling
   tables. A caller with no such container (`admin/_partials/sales_document_action_bar.html.twig`'s
   own two disclosures) instead adds `no-js-row-actions--overlay` alongside this class and gets the
   panel back as an absolutely-positioned overlay, so opening it does not shove the rest of the bar
   sideways or the page down (#650). #}
<style>
    /* `.row-action-menu` is `display: inline-flex`, which lays the summary and the panel out side
       by side and widens the Actions column instead of dropping the panel under the control. In
       flow, the panel belongs under the thing it belongs to. */
    .no-js-row-actions {
        display: inline-block;
        line-height: normal;
    }

    .no-js-row-actions > summary {
        cursor: pointer;
        list-style: none;
    }

    .no-js-row-actions > summary::-webkit-details-marker {
        display: none;
    }

    .no-js-row-actions[open] > .row-action-dropdown {
        display: grid;
        position: static;
        min-width: 0;
        margin-top: 6px;
    }

    .no-js-row-actions > .row-action-dropdown form {
        display: contents;
    }

    /* Opt-in modifier: FLOAT the panel over the page instead of pushing it down in flow. The
       in-flow default above exists purely for the row grids' `.table-scroll-region` clipping
       problem (see the docblock); a caller with no such scroll container to worry about — the
       sales document action bar's own two disclosures, so far — adds this class as well and gets
       an overlay instead, which is what stops opening one from shoving everything after it
       sideways or down (#650). `.row-action-menu` already gives the wrapper `position: relative`
       (app.css:6439), so the panel only has to go back to being `position: absolute` — the same
       box core's own JS-driven `.row-action-dropdown` already is before either modifier touches
       it. Both selectors tie on specificity with the in-flow rule above and this one wins by
       coming second in the cascade, but ONLY for the properties it sets — `min-width: 0` above is
       a property THIS rule leaves alone, so it still applies and starves the panel down toward its
       narrowest word instead of its widest item's full text. `width: max-content` here is the
       actual fix: it sizes the panel to its content regardless of that leftover `min-width: 0`,
       the same way `.row-action-fixed-drop` (app.css:12841) already does for its own dropdown. */
    .no-js-row-actions--overlay[open] > .row-action-dropdown {
        position: absolute;
        top: calc(100% + 8px);
        right: 0;
        z-index: 20;
        margin-top: 0;
        width: max-content;
        max-width: calc(100vw - 16px);
    }
</style>
